{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# E — Force measurement provenance into `[operator-attested]` evidence\n\n## Context / Problem\n\n`[operator-attested]` (ADR-0043) is a deliberate trust escape hatch, and the criteria guide states its\nevidence contract three separate times in the same shape:\n\n> treat a criterion … as sufficiently specified when its 'done' evidence lives outside the codebase and is a\n> concrete attestation recorded on the ticket (**a change id / vote outcome / timestamp**)\n\nThat contract demands provenance of the **attestation event**. It demands nothing about provenance of the\n**measurement**: which environment it was taken in, under which principal, at what privilege relative to\nproduction. The gate asks *\"is there an attestation?\"* — never *\"was it taken somewhere that could have\nexhibited the failure?\"*\n\nTwo escapes in the `061c-ecd1-8967-4a76` run went through exactly that hole, both recorded with attestations\nthat satisfied the contract as written:\n\n- **Wrong environment.** Bedrock caching / model-availability measurements taken in account `579718921998` /\n  `us-east-2` were recorded as settled fact for work targeting `896586841071` / `us-east-1` (the account named\n  by the Terraform backend `rebar-tfstate-896586841071` and by `infra/terraform/`). Caught by a human asking\n  \"is that this project's account?\", not by any gate.\n- **Privilege that cannot fail.** Live `bedrock:Converse` calls were offered as validation of a scoped IAM\n  grant, made under an identity holding `bedrock:*`. Evidence generated under broader privilege than\n  production is structurally unable to fail, so its success carries zero information. Role-scoped\n  `simulate-principal-policy` later proved the planned `bedrock:InvokeModel` grant would have `implicitDeny`-ed\n  `Converse` in production — feeding a cutover story (`eb6e-f43a-e86b-443a`) that has no fallback.\n\nNeither is a calibration miss. `verified_at_sha` pins the code world completely and the outside world not at\nall; plan-review runs before the evidence exists, and the completion-verifier accepts attested evidence on\ntrust by design. One gate cannot audit it; the other is designed not to.\n\n## Approach\n\nDo **not** try to make a gate observe the world. Force the measurement's context into the record as text, then\nlet the existing repo-grounded machinery find contradictions in it.\n\n**1. Extend the attested-evidence contract with measurement-provenance fields.** An `[operator-attested]`\ncriterion whose evidence comes from an external system must declare, alongside the existing change-id / vote /\ntimestamp:\n\n- `environment` — the concrete identifier (AWS account id, cluster, tenant, endpoint host);\n- `principal` — the identity the measurement ran as;\n- `privilege_posture` — one of `production-equivalent` / `broader` / `narrower`, plus a one-line justification\n  of why that posture can exhibit the failure the criterion rules out.\n\n**2. Enforce presence deterministically — zero LLM.** A `det_`-style check mirroring\n`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/det_operator_attested.py` (which already owns the canonical tag matcher). Presence\nand shape of the declaration is a string/structure question, so it belongs in the Layer-1 deterministic floor,\nconsistent with this repo's DET-first doctrine. It asserts the fields EXIST and are non-placeholder; it makes\nno judgement about whether they are *right*.\n\n**3. Let an LLM criterion judge the declaration against repo facts.** Once provenance exists as text, the\nwrong-account escape becomes a **repo-checkable contradiction**: a reviewer that can read `infra/` can flag\n\"the plan attests measurements from account X, but `infra/terraform/versions.tf`'s backend and the IaC name\naccount Y\". That is exactly the kind of grounded probe the gate's strongest criteria (E4, G1G2, T3, T10)\nalready perform — it simply had nothing to read before.\n\n**4. Add the generalized rule as a criterion**, shaped as declaration-completeness rather than world-observation:\n\n> Evidence produced under broader privilege, or in a different environment, than the thing being tested is not\n> evidence.\n\nStructurally this is the same shape as T5c's undeclared-access-posture hunt, aimed at **verification plans**\ninstead of designs. It is the world-grounded generalization of E4's \"cached knowledge is not a substitute for a\nprobe\", and of what mutation testing does to a vacuous test.\n\n**Calibration:** per ADR-0054, it enters **advisory**; corpus replay decides promotion to blocking. It must not\nship blocking on first landing.\n\n## Explicitly rejected\n\n- **Making the completion-verifier re-run world measurements.** Expensive, flaky, and recursive — the\n  verifier's own credentials carry the same privilege-masking problem, so it would validate using the\n  instrument under suspicion. The cheap, honest lever is forcing provenance into the record.\n- **Treating \"reported green while typecheck was red\" as a gate gap.** That is reporting discipline, already\n  covered by the orchestration rule that agents report gate output faithfully rather than narrating success.\n  Filing it here would be mis-attribution and would dilute this story's scope.\n- **Wording changes to the existing contract.** The rule was followed and still failed; emphasis is not the\n  defect (see the parent epic's framing).\n- **Catching a FALSE declaration (the honest-declaration limit, G6).** This mechanism closes the\n  **undeclared** hole, not the **lying** one. `environment` is refutable against repo facts (the IaC\n  backend names an account), so a wrong-account declaration becomes a repo-checkable contradiction.\n  `principal` and `privilege_posture` are NOT repo-refutable: an author who runs under `bedrock:*` and\n  writes `privilege_posture: production-equivalent` is not caught, and nothing in a repo-grounded gate\n  could catch them. That class is scoped OUT by ADR-0043's trust boundary and this story INHERITS it\n  deliberately rather than claiming to close it. What the fields buy for the non-refutable two is\n  narrower and still real: they make the posture claim **explicit, attributable and reviewable by a\n  human**, converting a silent omission into a recorded assertion someone can challenge. The story's\n  prose must not overclaim beyond that.\n\n## Scope\n\n**Concrete names pinned by this plan** (no placeholders — every proving command below runs as written):\n\n- new criterion id: **`project.measurement-provenance`**\n- new deterministic check module: **`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/det_measurement_provenance.py`**\n\n**In scope**\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/det_measurement_provenance.py` — a NEW `det_`-style deterministic\n  presence/shape check for the provenance fields, a pure leaf alongside `det_operator_attested.py`\n  (which keeps sole ownership of the canonical `[operator-attested]` tag matcher; the new module\n  IMPORTS that matcher rather than restating it, so \"tagged\" stays single-sourced).\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/det_floor.py` — surface the new lint through `p6_ac_quality`, exactly as\n  the sibling operator-attested lint is surfaced. `p6_ac_quality` is ADVISORY and never blocks.\n- `.rebar/criteria_routing.json` — define and activate `project.measurement-provenance` at\n  `default_posture: \"advisory\"`, using the SAME overlay mechanism the existing `project.*` criteria use,\n  and pinned at **`\"exec\": \"AGENT\"`**. The tier is load-bearing, not cosmetic: `AGENT` is the only\n  tool-using tier (`registry.AGENT_TIER`, \"one tool-using agent loop each\"), and the criterion's whole job\n  is to READ an in-repo anchor (`infra/terraform/versions.tf`) and compare it to the declared\n  `environment`. At `1-TURN` it would have no filesystem tooling and the contradiction probe could not run.\n- **`.rebar/prompts/plan-review-project-measurement-provenance.md` — a NEW rubric prompt file.** This is\n  REQUIRED, not optional: `registry.build_descriptor` resolves every activated non-DET `project.<name>`\n  criterion's rubric body via `criterion_prompt_id()` -> `.rebar/prompts/plan-review-project-<name>.md`,\n  and the routing entry's `checklist` is empty for every existing project criterion. Without this file the\n  criterion raises `PromptNotFound` and the LLM half of the mechanism cannot load at all. The rubric must\n  state the three decision rules in one place: (a) declared `environment` vs the in-repo IaC anchor —\n  MISMATCH is a finding; (b) NO comparable anchor in the repo — NO finding (see the no-anchor case);\n  (c) `privilege_posture: broader` on a criterion verifying a scoped permission — a finding;\n  `production-equivalent` / `narrower` — no finding. It follows the shape of the three existing\n  `.rebar/prompts/plan-review-project-*.md` files.\n- `docs/adr/` — an ADR recording the amended evidence contract, citing ADR-0043 as the shape it changes.\n- Tests + corpus/replay fixtures for the ADR-0054 calibration.\n\n**Explicitly OUT of scope — the shipped contract text is NOT edited** (this is the correction that\nkeeps AC-6 honest). The three `OPERATOR-ATTESTED RULE` statements do NOT live in the docs: they are\nshipped reviewer prompts at `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/plan_review_F1.md`, `plan_review_E2.md` and\n`plan_review_E6.md`, with a fourth contract-text site at `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/coach_moves.py`\n(coach move `14`). `docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md` is GENERATED from the registry and parity-gated\n(`registry.validate_criteria_guide`), so it cannot be hand-edited independently. Editing any of those\nfour sites would change the contract for EVERY rebar client — the exact outcome AC-6 forbids. Therefore\nthis story amends the contract in the ADR + the project overlay ONLY, and leaves all four shipped sites\nbyte-identical. Rolling the requirement into the shipped default set is a SEPARATE, later decision that\nADR-0054 corpus replay must justify first.\n\n## Testing\n\n- **Deterministic check, unit tier**: an attested criterion WITHOUT provenance fields is flagged; one WITH them\n  passes; a placeholder value (`TBD`, `<account>`, empty) is flagged. No LLM in the loop.\n- **Contradiction detection, contract tier**: a plan declaring account X against a repo whose Terraform backend\n  names account Y produces a finding. **This is the regression test for the actual escape** — build the fixture\n  from the real one (`579718921998` declared, `896586841071` in `infra/terraform/versions.tf`).\n- **Contrast case**: a plan whose declared environment MATCHES the repo's IaC produces no finding — otherwise\n  the criterion is noise on every correctly-attested ticket.\n- **Privilege-posture case**: a criterion verifying a *scoped* permission while declaring\n  `privilege_posture: broader` is flagged; `production-equivalent` or `narrower` is not. This is the\n  `bedrock:*` escape in miniature.\n- **No-anchor fallback case (T8)**: a plan whose declared `environment` has NO comparable in-repo anchor —\n  a repo with no Terraform/IaC backend, or a non-AWS identifier (cluster / tenant / endpoint host) with no\n  in-repo counterpart — produces NO finding. Absence of an anchor is NOT a contradiction; the criterion\n  fires only on an actual declared-vs-repo MISMATCH. Without this the criterion would nag every repo that\n  has no IaC.\n- **Grandfathering case (T4)**: an EXISTING `[operator-attested]` criterion predating this contract (no\n  provenance fields at all) is surfaced as an advisory coaching string and does NOT block a claim or a\n  close. This is the regression test that the contract change ships without a migration break.\n- **Calibration**: corpus replay per ADR-0054 measuring false-positive rate before any promotion from advisory.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] an `[operator-attested]` criterion lacking `environment` / `principal` / `privilege_posture` is flagged by\n      a DETERMINISTIC check (no LLM), and a test asserts the check makes no judgement about correctness — only\n      presence and non-placeholder shape\n- [ ] a test asserts the wrong-account escape is now catchable: a plan declaring one account against a repo\n      whose Terraform backend names another produces a finding, built from the real `579718921998` vs\n      `896586841071` case\n- [ ] a CONTRAST test asserts a correctly-declared plan produces NO finding, so the criterion does not fire on\n      every well-formed ticket\n- [ ] a test asserts a scoped-permission criterion declaring `privilege_posture: broader` is flagged, and that\n      `production-equivalent` is not — the `bedrock:*` case\n- [ ] the new criterion ships **advisory**, never blocking on first landing (ADR-0054), and the ticket records\n      the corpus-replay false-positive rate that would justify promotion\n- [ ] the criterion rides the `.rebar/` project overlay, not the shipped default criteria set:\n      `grep -rn 'project.measurement-provenance' src/rebar/` prints nothing (exit 1), while the overlay\n      carries BOTH the definition and the activation — asserted structurally (not by line-counting, which\n      a compact JSON layout would defeat):\n      `python3 -c \"import json;d=json.load(open('.rebar/criteria_routing.json'));c='project.measurement-provenance';assert c in d['plan_review'];assert 'plan_review' in d['activate'][c];assert d['plan_review'][c]['default_posture']=='advisory';assert d['plan_review'][c]['exec']=='AGENT'\"`\n      exits 0 — no other rebar client gains a new BLOCKING criterion\n- [ ] the shipped contract text is untouched, proving no cross-client behaviour change:\n      `git diff --exit-code origin/main -- src/rebar/llm/reviewers/plan_review_F1.md\n      src/rebar/llm/reviewers/plan_review_E2.md src/rebar/llm/reviewers/plan_review_E6.md\n      src/rebar/llm/plan_review/coach_moves.py docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md` exits 0\n- [ ] the DETERMINISTIC check ships ADVISORY: a test asserts an existing `[operator-attested]` criterion\n      with NO provenance fields yields a coaching string but does NOT block — surfaced through\n      `p6_ac_quality`, which never blocks — so tickets predating the contract keep claiming and closing\n      with no migration step\n- [ ] a test asserts the NO-ANCHOR case produces no finding: a declared `environment` with no comparable\n      in-repo anchor (no IaC backend, or a non-AWS cluster/tenant identifier) is NOT flagged, so the\n      criterion cannot nag repos that have no infrastructure-as-code\n- [ ] the criterion's rubric prompt EXISTS and is loadable, so the LLM half cannot silently fail to build:\n      `test -f .rebar/prompts/plan-review-project-measurement-provenance.md` exits 0, and a test builds the\n      criterion descriptor for `project.measurement-provenance` without raising `PromptNotFound`\n- [ ] an ADR records the amended evidence contract and cites ADR-0043 as the shape it changes\n- [ ] the rejected alternative stays rejected — the new check never re-runs a world measurement:\n      `grep -rnE 'boto3|botocore|subprocess|requests|urllib|socket|httpx' src/rebar/llm/plan_review/det_measurement_provenance.py`\n      prints nothing (exit 1), i.e. the module is a pure-stdlib text check with no network or shell reach\n- [ ] [operator-attested] `make lint`, `make typecheck` and the FULL gating suite pass, evidenced by the Gerrit\n      change id and its `Verified +1` recorded in a comment on this ticket — and, dogfooding this story's own\n      rule, that comment states the environment, principal and privilege posture of the run"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785904820501859001,"uuid":"a1baa330-4110-4a7f-b9c8-4f2ca7a31668"}